insert into public.practice_exercises (id, slug, title, summary, skill, band_group, question_types, duration_minutes, question_count, passage, audio_path, audio_mime, audio_bytes, is_published) values ('d8995187-5143-5a8b-88a1-099ebea8613d', 'the-cooling-power-of-trees', 'The cooling power of trees', 'Đọc bằng chứng về tán cây, nhiệt độ và lựa chọn quy hoạch.', 'reading', '6_7', array['multiple_choice', 'true_false_not_given', 'matching_headings', 'gap_fill']::text[], 18, 6, array['A. On a summer afternoon, the difference between a tree-lined street and a bare car park can be striking. Shade blocks solar radiation, while water released through leaves removes heat from the surrounding air. These processes are well understood, yet predicting the cooling effect of a particular planting scheme remains difficult. The result depends on the size and health of trees, the arrangement of buildings and the availability of water. A single figure describing the benefit of urban trees can therefore be misleading.', 'B. Researchers in the fictional city of Mereton installed temperature sensors along twelve streets. Rather than comparing neighbourhood averages, they placed instruments at the height of a pedestrian''s head. Streets with continuous tree cover were generally cooler during the afternoon, but the difference narrowed after sunset. The researchers cautioned that their study covered only one summer. It demonstrated a local pattern, not a universal promise that the same planting plan would produce identical results elsewhere.', 'C. Choosing a species involves trade-offs. A fast-growing tree may create shade quickly but require considerable water. A drought-tolerant species may survive with less irrigation while developing a smaller canopy. Underground pipes, narrow pavements and maintenance budgets can limit what is feasible. Planners increasingly favour a mixture of species: diversity reduces the risk that a single disease will remove an entire street''s shade. It also makes maintenance less predictable because different trees need attention at different times.', 'D. Distribution matters as much as the number of trees planted. In many cities, areas with the highest temperatures also have the least canopy cover. If new planting goes mainly to locations where residents can contribute money, existing inequalities may deepen. Mereton''s council consequently prioritised routes used by people walking to schools and clinics. Its programme included funding for watering young trees over several years. Announcing how many trees had been planted would have been easier than checking how many survived, but survival was the measure the council chose.', 'E. Trees cannot solve every problem associated with extreme heat. Reflective roofs, ventilation, accessible drinking water and shaded public spaces remain important. The evidence supports incorporating trees into a wider strategy rather than treating planting as a substitute for other measures. A successful programme combines realistic biological expectations with a clear account of who will benefit and who will maintain the trees.']::text[], null, null, null, true);
